{"id":"6fd9573a-4521-4b67-a5df-e4a1970369b0","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885424,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    # dp[i] maps cost -> (score, total_length, indices_tuple)\\n    dp = {0: (0.0, 0, ())}\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        new_dp = {}\\n        for cost, (score, length, indices) in dp.items():\\n            # skip item i\\n            if cost not in new_dp:\\n                new_dp[cost] = (score, length, indices)\\n            else:\\n                cur = new_dp[cost]\\n                if (score > cur[0] or\\n                    (score == cur[0] and length < cur[1]) or\\n                    (score == cur[0] and length == cur[1] and indices < cur[2])):\\n                    new_dp[cost] = (score, length, indices)\\n            # take item i\\n            new_cost = cost + lengths[i]\\n            if new_cost <= budget:\\n                new_score = score + scores[i]\\n                new_length = length + lengths[i]\\n                new_indices = indices + (i,)\\n                if new_cost not in new_dp:\\n                    new_dp[new_cost] = (new_score, new_length, new_indices)\\n                else:\\n                    cur = new_dp[new_cost]\\n                    if (new_score > cur[0] or\\n                        (new_score == cur[0] and new_length < cur[1]) or\\n                        (new_score == cur[0] and new_length == cur[1] and new_indices < cur[2])):\\n                        new_dp[new_cost] = (new_score, new_length, new_indices)\\n        dp = new_dp\\n    # find best among all costs in dp\\n    best_score = None\\n    best_length = None\\n    best_indices = None\\n    for cost, (score, length, indices) in dp.items():\\n        if (best_score is None or\\n            score > best_score or\\n            (score == best_score and length < best_length) or\\n            (score == best_score and length == best_length and indices < best_indices)):\\n            best_score = score\\n            best_length = length\\n            best_indices = indices\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_indices\\\": list(best_indices),\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": best_length,\\n        \\\"total_score\\\": best_score\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task NLP-CSE-02. The task is to select sentences (each with a length in tokens and a score) to maximize total score subject to a total token budget, preserving document order (i.e., we can only select a prefix? Actually the problem says \"preserving document order\" and \"required query context\". But from the description: \"Select and reconstruct context-scored sentences under an exact token budget while preserving document order and required query context.\" Also \"Enumerate sentence inclusion with a zero-one dynamic program over token cost.\" It's essentially a knapsack where each sentence has a cost (length) and a value (score), and we want to maximize sum of scores subject to budget. However, we need to preserve document order? In a typical 0-1 knapsack, order doesn't matter; we can pick any subset. But \"preserving document order\" might mean that we must select sentences in the order they appear, i.e., we can only select a contiguous subsequence? Or perhaps it means that we can only skip sentences but cannot reorder; i.e., any subset is allowed as long as we keep the order of selected sentences same as original. That's just a subset, no reordering. The phrase \"preserving document order\" typically means that we cannot reorder selected sentences; they appear in the same order as in the document. That's automatically satisfied if we select a subset and output indices in increasing order. So it's just a standard knapsack with costs.\n\nBut there is also \"required query context\". Might refer to always including the first sentence? Not clear from payloads. The payloads don't have any separate query indicator. The fixture payload has `lengths`, `scores`, `budget`. So we just need to pick a subset of indices to maximize sum of scores while sum of lengths <= budget. And we need to break ties: \"Break equal utility by fewer used tokens and then lexicographic source indices.\" So if two solutions have same total score, choose the one with smaller total tokens used; if still tie, choose the one with lexicographically smaller index list (i.e., compare first index, if equal then second, etc.). Need to output `selected_indices` (list of integers, presumably 0-based? The description says \"lexicographic source indices\" and input has lengths and scores arrays, indices are from 0 to n-1. We'll output indices in increasing order (document order). Also need `tokens_used` (integer sum of lengths of selected sentences) and `total_score` (sum of scores).\n\nWe must implement dynamic programming. Since n is not huge (maybe up to a few hundred), we can do standard DP over cost. But we need to reconstruct solution with tie-breaking. DP will keep best value for each state (i, c) = max score using first i sentences with total cost <= c. Then reconstruct. But tie-breaking: prefer fewer tokens if equal score. That's naturally handled if we iterate costs from low to high and when updating, if we have equal score, we need to prefer the state with fewer tokens. But the DP stores max score, not token count. We can store also the token count of the best solution for each state, or we can incorporate it into a tuple (score, -tokens_used) to compare lexicographically. Since we want to maximize score then minimize tokens, we can use a pair (score, -tokens) and compare lexicographically. That will automatically favor higher score, and if equal score, larger -tokens (i.e., fewer tokens). Also need lexicographic indices for final tie among same score and same tokens. So we can store for each state the best tuple (score, -tokens) and also the list of indices? That would be heavy. Better to store DP values and reconstruct after computing best value. We can store for each (i,c) the best value, and also a predecessor to reconstruct. For tie-breaking, we can define a comparator that compares (score, -tokens_used, list_of_indices_lexicographically). But during DP, we can compute the best for each state by comparing with candidate from including current sentence. We need to compare the resulting tuple of the candidate versus existing best. Since we need lexicographically smallest index list as last tie, we can incorporate that into the state comparison. However, storing the full index list for each state is expensive but n small, maybe acceptable. But we can also reconstruct by going through DP decisions and at each step if there are multiple solutions with same value, we can pick the one with fewer tokens and then lexicographically smaller indices by backtracking with a priority. Simpler: Do DP that stores best score and best count of tokens (total length) for each (i,c). Then after DP, we know the best score and best tokens. Then we need to find the lexicographically smallest index list among those with that score and that token count. We can do a second pass: iterate through DP table to reconstruct the set of selected indices that achieve the best. But we need the lexicographically smallest. We can reconstruct greedily: for each position i from 0 to n-1, decide whether to include it or not, based on whether there exists a solution with the remaining budget and desired remaining score. Since we know the target total score and total tokens, we can check if including the current sentence can lead to a completion that matches the best. We need to choose the smallest index list, so we should try to include if possible and if it doesn't force a worse solution. This is standard lexicographically smallest reconstruction for knapsack.\n\nSimplify: Since constraints are small, we can enumerate all subsets? That's 2^n, too large. n can be up to maybe 100? Actually from fixtures, n is 5 or 6. But could be larger. We'll implement DP with bitmask? Better to implement DP with dictionary or list of dictionaries for each i. We'll have DP[i][c] = max score achievable with first i items and cost exactly c? Or cost <= c? Use cost exactly to avoid huge arrays. But we need to handle budget up to maybe large? lengths can be up to maybe 10? Not sure. But we can use dict for each i, storing cost -> best (score, tokens) and also a list of indices? Not needed. We'll store the best score and token count, and later reconstruct.\n\nImplement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lengths = case['lengths']\n    scores = case['scores']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(lengths)\n\n    # DP: dict mapping total_cost -> (best_score, best_tokens) for states up to some i.\n    # But we need to know which items used? We'll store for each cost the best (score, tokens) and also the last item? Actually for reconstruction we can store the decision as a predecessor.\n    # Use list of dict for each i: dp[i][c] = (score, tokens, prev_cost, included?) or store the best tuple.\n    # Simpler: We'll do DP using dictionary of cost -> (score, tokens) for each stage, and also store a predecessor cost and inclusion flag.\n    # But we need to compare using (score, -tokens). So we store (score, tokens) and compare with (score, -tokens) lexicographically.\n    # For each item i, we update new dict from old.\n    # Then after processing all items, find best score and token count among costs <= budget. We'll pick the best according to (score, -tokens). Then reconstruct by backtracking stored decisions.\n\n    dp = {0: (0.0, 0, None, None)}  # cost -> (score, tokens, prev_cost, included) # but for cost=0 we have no previous, we can store prev_cost=None, included=None\n    # Actually we need to store for each cost the best state after processing i items.\n    # We'll have a list of dicts: states[i] = dict mapping cost-> (score, tokens, prev_cost, included_i)\n    # But we can just keep one dict and update in reverse order to avoid using current item multiple times? We'll do it per item and create new dict.\n\n    states = [None] * (n+1)\n    states[0] = {0: (0.0, 0, None, None)}  # (score, total_length, prev_cost, decision) decision: 0 for not taken, 1 for taken? But we need to know whether item i was taken to reconstruct. We'll store the cost before including this item (prev_cost) and whether it was included. So from a state with cost c, we know if it came from previous item without inclusion (prev_cost = c, included=False) or with inclusion (prev_cost = c - lengths[i], included=True). But we need to know inclusion flag to indicate if item i is selected. So store (score, total_length, prev_cost, included) where included is bool.\n\n    For i from 0 to n-1:\n        new_dp = {}\n        for cost, (score, length, prev_cost, included) in states[i].items():\n            # skip this item\n            if cost not in new_dp or (score > new_dp[cost][0] or (score == new_dp[cost][0] and length < new_dp[cost][1])):\n                new_dp[cost] = (score, length, cost, False)  # prev_cost same as cost, included False\n            # take this item if within budget\n            new_cost = cost + lengths[i]\n            if new_cost <= budget:\n                new_score = score + scores[i]\n                new_length = length + lengths[i]\n                # compare with existing at new_cost\n                if new_cost not in new_dp or (new_score > new_dp[new_cost][0] or (new_score == new_dp[new_cost][0] and new_length < new_dp[new_cost][1])):\n                    new_dp[new_cost] = (new_score, new_length, cost, True)\n        states[i+1] = new_dp\n\n    # After processing all items, find best final state with cost <= budget (but our states are exact costs, we only have costs that are sums of lengths, so they are within budget if we only added when <= budget. But we must consider that we might have not used full budget. The best state is among those costs <= budget. We'll just find best among all costs in states[n] using (score, -length) lexicographically.\n    best_score = None\n    best_length = None\n    best_cost = None\n    for cost, (score, length, _, _) in states[n].items():\n        if best_score is None or (score > best_score) or (score == best_score and length < best_length):\n            best_score = score\n            best_length = length\n            best_cost = cost\n\n    # Now reconstruct indices list by backtracking from best_cost at state n.\n    indices = []\n    cur_cost = best_cost\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        # Get state from states[i+1] at cur_cost\n        _, _, prev_cost, included = states[i+1][cur_cost]\n        if included:\n            indices.append(i)\n            cur_cost = prev_cost\n        # else: cur_cost unchanged\n    indices.reverse()\n    \n    # Return\n    return {\n        \"selected_indices\": indices,\n        \"tokens_used\": best_length,\n        \"total_score\": best_score\n    }\n\nBut note: Tie-breaking also mentions lexicographic source indices. Our reconstruction as described might not guarantee lexicographically smallest indices if there are multiple solutions with same score and same tokens. The DP stores only one state per cost based on (score, -length) comparison. But there could be multiple ways to achieve same score and same length at a particular cost, and we only kept one (the first encountered due to the comparison not considering indices). However, we need to ensure that among solutions with same score and same length, we pick the one with lexicographically smaller indices. Our reconstruction also uses the stored decision chain, which may not be the lexicographically smallest. To guarantee, we need to incorporate lexicographic comparison of indices into the DP state selection. But that's complicated. An alternative: after DP, we can perform a second DP that finds the lexicographically smallest index list for the best score and best length. Since we have the best score and best length, we can do a forward greedy selection: for each index i from 0 to n-1, decide whether to include it if there exists a solution using the remaining budget and achieving the remaining score, with the constraint that the total length and score match exactly. This is similar to reconstructing lexicographically smallest solution in coin change. We can do a DP that tells us, for a given index i and remaining budget and required remaining score, whether it's possible to achieve exactly that. Then we can greedily decide to include if possible, because including earlier gives lexicographically smaller (since we prefer smaller indices). But we also need to consider that we have to match the exact total score and length. We know best_score and best_length are the optimal values. But there could be multiple solutions achieving that exact score and length. We want lexicographically smallest indices.\n\nBetter approach: We can incorporate lexicographic ordering into the DP state by storing for each (i, c) the best tuple (score, -length, indices) as a list? That would be heavy. But we can store a string representation? Not ideal.\n\nGiven that the fixtures only have up to 6 items, the DP with full index list per state is feasible. We can store for each state the list of indices (or at least the decision path) as part of the value. But that would increase memory but n small. However, we don't know what n will be in other tests. It could be larger (maybe up to 1000?). The problem description is from an evaluation platform, likely n up to a few dozen. We can implement a more robust greedy reconstruction using a feasibility DP.\n\nFeasibility DP: after we know best_score and best_length, we can compute a 3D boolean DP: possible[i][c][s] where i is number of items processed, c is cost, s is score. But score can be float, not integer. So we cannot use exact score matching. We need to handle floats. That's problematic.\n\nAlternative: Use floating point tolerance? But the problem says total_score is number (float). In output schema, total_score is number (float). So scores are floats. We cannot compare exact equality due to floating point errors. We need to handle this carefully. The output should be the same as computed by DP. Since we compute DP using floats, we can store the exact score value. When comparing, we need to consider that two solutions might have the same score within tolerance. But the problem likely expects exact matching because the scores are given with many decimal places. However, due to floating point addition, there could be slight differences. To avoid issues, we can treat scores as Python floats and use exact comparison (since the number of additions is small, the result is deterministic). For tie-breaking, we need to compare equality of scores. We can compute the sum exactly using decimal? But we are allowed only standard library. We'll assume floats are fine.\n\nFor greedy reconstruction with floats, we can compute the remaining score needed. But we need to check if there exists a subset achieving exactly that remaining score. That's like subset sum with floats, which is not robust.\n\nGiven these complications, it's simpler to store the full index list in the DP state for each (cost) at each stage, but only retain the best according to (score, -length, indices) lexicographically. Since n is typically small (perhaps up to 30), the number of costs is bounded by budget (which could be up to maybe 1000? lengths are small integers, so costs are integer). The number of states is O(n * budget). Storing a full list of indices for each state would be O(n * budget * n) memory, which could be large if n=1000 and budget=1000 -> 1e6 * 1000 = 1e9, too big. But we can store the indices list only for the best state at each cost, and the list is the indices of selected sentences. That list can be up to n length, so memory is O(n * budget). For n=1000, budget=1000, that's 1e6 entries each storing a list of up to 1000 indices -> too large. But in practice, budget may be limited and lengths are small, so number of selected sentences is limited. Still, we should aim for a more efficient approach.\n\nWe can instead store for each state the decision (included or not) and the previous cost, and then after DP we can reconstruct the lexicographically smallest by doing a second pass that tries to include earlier items if possible, using the DP values to check feasibility. For feasibility checking with floats, we can store the best score achievable from a given state (i, remaining_budget) with the remaining items. We can compute a DP that stores the maximum score achievable from index i with remaining budget b. That's standard knapsack from suffix. Then we can greedily decide: for i from 0 to n-1, if we can include item i and still achieve the best total score (or same score with fewer tokens?) we include it. But we need to consider tie-breaking with tokens and indices. We know the best overall score (max_score) and best tokens (min_tokens_for_max_score). Let's denote target_score = max_score, target_tokens = best_tokens. In greedy reconstruction, we want to find the lexicographically smallest index list that yields exactly target_score and target_tokens. We can do forward DP: For each i from 0 to n-1, decide whether to include i. We'll maintain remaining_budget and remaining_score (target_score_remaining) and remaining_tokens? Actually we need to achieve exactly total length = best_tokens. So we have two constraints: sum lengths = best_tokens, sum scores = best_score. That is a constrained exact knapsack. We can compute DP from the end: suffix[i][c] = maximum score achievable from items i..n-1 with cost exactly c? Or maximum score? But we need exact cost and exact score. Since scores are floats, exact match is problematic. However, we can use the original DP values: we have dp_forward[i][c] = best (score, length) for first i items with cost c. And we need to combine with suffix? Or we can use the DP to check if including item i leads to a feasible solution: there must exist a combination of items 0..i-1 and i+1..n-1 such that total cost and total score match target. We can precompute prefix and suffix DP.\n\nGiven the complexity and small n likely, storing full index list per state is acceptable. But to be safe and meet the expected solution pattern, we'll implement a DP that for each i and each cost, stores the best tuple (score, length, indices) where indices is a tuple of selected indices (sorted). We will compare lexicographically: first compare score (higher better), then compare length (lower better), then compare indices list lexicographically (lower better). Since we need to compare indices, we can store them as tuple. When merging, we compare using Python's tuple comparison: (score, -length, indices) vs (other_score, -other_length, other_indices). Since score is a float, we need to handle equal scores. We'll use exact equality. For indices, we can compare the tuples directly. This is acceptable for n up to maybe 30-50, budget up to maybe 200, leading to a few thousand states each with a tuple of indices of length up to n. But memory might be okay if n is small. We can also store only the best state per cost and per i, discarding previous i.\n\nImplement with list of dicts:\n\nstates = [None] * (n+1)\nstates[0] = {0: (0.0, 0, ())}  # (score, length, indices_tuple)\n\nfor i in range(n):\n    new_dp = {}\n    for cost, (score, length, indices) in states[i].items():\n        # option skip\n        if cost not in new_dp:\n            new_dp[cost] = (score, length, indices)\n        else:\n            existing = new_dp[cost]\n            # compare (score, -length, indices)\n            if (score > existing[0] or\n                (score == existing[0] and length < existing[1]) or\n                (score == existing[0] and length == existing[1] and indices < existing[2])):\n                new_dp[cost] = (score, length, indices)\n        # option take\n        new_cost = cost + lengths[i]\n        if new_cost <= budget:\n            new_score = score + scores[i]\n            new_length = length + lengths[i]\n            new_indices = indices + (i,)  # keep sorted because we add in order\n            if new_cost not in new_dp:\n                new_dp[new_cost] = (new_score, new_length, new_indices)\n            else:\n                existing = new_dp[new_cost]\n                if (new_score > existing[0] or\n                    (new_score == existing[0] and new_length < existing[1]) or\n                    (new_score == existing[0] and new_length == existing[1] and new_indices < existing[2])):\n                    new_dp[new_cost] = (new_score, new_length, new_indices)\n    states[i+1] = new_dp\n\nThen find best among states[n] using same comparison.\n\nThis is straightforward and handles tie-breaking correctly. But we must ensure that we are not duplicating states across different i with same cost but different indices? Actually each cost at each i corresponds to a set of selected items from first i items. Since we always add items in order, the indices tuple is naturally sorted. The comparison is lexicographic tuple comparison, which matches requirement.\n\nNow, we need to return selected_indices as list, tokens_used as integer, total_score as number.\n\nAlso handle edge case: no sentence fits? Then selected_indices empty, tokens_used=0, total_score=0.0? But there is no requirement to always select something. The knapsack always allows empty set. So we initialize dp with empty set.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNote: The input case is a dict with keys lengths, scores, budget. There may be additional keys like _case_nonce, but we ignore.\n\nWe'll write the function and then wrap it in the required JSON envelope.\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1134,"completion_tokens":5475,"total_tokens":6609,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4893},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":110},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}